# PART 3: ENFORCE AND RECORD PAYMENT POLICY ACCEPTANCE

Do not implement the no-refund policy as a purely decorative checkbox.

It must be part of the actual payment initialization workflow.

### Frontend enforcement

All customer payment initialization requests should include an explicit acceptance value, for example:

`paymentPolicyAccepted: true`

This must be sent only after the customer has actively selected the checkbox and clicked the payment action.

### Backend enforcement

Update:

`netlify/functions/paystack-initialize.ts`

The server must independently validate that the authenticated payment request includes valid Payment Policy acceptance.

If acceptance is missing or false, reject the request and do not initialize a Paystack transaction.

Do not rely exclusively on frontend button disabling.

### Acceptance audit information

Preserve the acceptance in Paystack transaction metadata and in the final verified payment record.

Record appropriate information such as:

- `paymentPolicyAccepted`
- `paymentPolicyVersion`
- `paymentPolicyText`
- `paymentPolicyAcceptedAt`
- `paymentPolicyAcceptedBy`

The acceptance timestamp should be generated by the trusted server rather than blindly trusting a browser-supplied timestamp.

Use the authenticated user ID.

Update:

`api/_lib/payment-reconciliation.ts`

Ensure the relevant acceptance metadata is transferred into the final payment document when Paystack successfully reconciles the transaction.

This should provide an auditable record of the policy version the customer accepted.

If the policy is updated in the future, introduce a new policy version and require fresh acceptance.

### Important safeguards

Do not:

- Pre-check the checkbox.
- Automatically accept on behalf of the customer.
- Allow a direct API request to bypass acceptance.
- Initialize payment when policy acceptance is missing.
- Mark failed or abandoned transactions as successfully paid.
- Change the existing payment verification or reconciliation rules.

Treat the checkbox as an explicit policy acknowledgment, not as a claim that it overrides any mandatory legal rights.

